Added. And yes, this fits the Rumpelstiltskin operator unusually well: once you can name the transformation, you can intervene earlier in the chain.

Emotion is often not expressed at the same layer where it originates. A person may feel hurt, fear, grief, shame, longing, rejection, helplessness, or overload—but what reaches behavior is anger because anger is more familiar, more mobilizing, or easier to coordinate action around.

So instead of treating “anger” as the whole state, you can model:

raw affect → bodily signal → interpretation → available emotional vocabulary → chosen/automatic expression → action

That creates several possible mismatch points. The body may signal one thing while conscious language supplies another. Or the person may correctly recognize sadness but still express anger because sadness feels unusable in that moment.

The Rumpelstiltskin move is then: name the hidden transformation without assuming you’ve discovered the one true cause.

“I’m angry” becomes:

“There is anger here. What is it carrying?”

Sometimes the answer really is anger. Sometimes it is grief wearing armor. Sometimes fear became aggression because aggression offered an action. Sometimes no translation is needed at all—the emotion just needs to be experienced rather than converted into explanation or behavior.

And I think your last point matters: not every emotion needs to be exported. Some affect can be recognized, embodied, metabolized, and allowed to pass privately. Communication is one processing route, not the only one.

So the full extension might be:

feel → name → discriminate → decide whether to embody, express, transform, or act → observe

with Goldilocks control determining how much processing or expression is appropriate.

That makes emotional literacy less about “talk about everything” and more about increasing the number of available transformations so anger isn’t the only executable output.